    <article class="docs-prose">
      <h1 id="stage-10-keys" class="group scroll-mt-20">Stage 10: Keys<a href="#stage-10-keys" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h1>
      <p>Define keys for the product name, version, download URL, and selected topics. A <code>&lt;keyword keyref="product-name"/&gt;</code> uses text from a key definition; an <code>&lt;xref keyref="install"/&gt;</code> uses a key to identify a link target. Updating the definition changes each reference that resolves to it.</p>
      <p>Store product values in <code>keydefs-product.ditamap</code> and include that map with <code>&lt;mapref&gt;</code>. Define topic keys with <code>@keys</code> on the guide's existing <code>&lt;topicref&gt;</code> elements.</p>
      <p><strong>Time:</strong> about 30 minutes. <strong>You need:</strong> stage 09 complete.</p>
      <p>Recorded output below is an example. File counts, paths, and stage numbers can differ. <a href="/dogsbay-xml-dita-tutorial-docs/start-here/run-the-gate">Check your work</a> to see the result for your own project.</p>
      <h2 id="step-1-define-the-product-keys" class="group scroll-mt-20">Step 1: Define the product keys<a href="#step-1-define-the-product-keys" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h2>
      <Steps>
      <Step>
      <strong>Create </strong><code>keydefs-product.ditamap</code> in <code>my-audacity-guide</code>. In the <strong>Explorer</strong>, right-click <code>my-audacity-guide</code>, choose <strong>New File</strong>, and enter <code>keydefs-product.ditamap</code>. In the <strong>New XML Document</strong> dialog, click <strong>Key Definition Map</strong>. The template contains two sample key definitions:
      <CodeBlock code={"<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n<!DOCTYPE map PUBLIC \"-//OASIS//DTD DITA Map//EN\" \"map.dtd\">\n\n<map>\n  <title>Key Definitions</title>\n  <keydef keys=\"installing\" href=\"topic.dita\"/>\n  <keydef keys=\"product-name\">\n    <topicmeta>\n      <keywords>\n        <keyword>Product Name</keyword>\n      </keywords>\n    </topicmeta>\n  </keydef>\n</map>"} lang="xml" />
      <p>Replace the title with <code>Product key definitions</code>. Delete the sample <code>installing</code> key definition, because this map defines text and URL keys only; step 2 keys the topics in the guide map. Keep the <code>product-name</code> key definition and replace <code>Product Name</code> with <code>Audacity</code>, then add the other three key definitions. Save the file, so that the build sees it. If you use another editor, create the file and type the finished listing. The finished map:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n<!DOCTYPE map PUBLIC \"-//OASIS//DTD DITA Map//EN\" \"map.dtd\">\n\n<map>\n  <title>Product key definitions</title>\n\n  <keydef keys=\"product-name\">\n    <topicmeta>\n      <keywords>\n        <keyword>Audacity</keyword>\n      </keywords>\n    </topicmeta>\n  </keydef>\n  <keydef keys=\"product-version\">\n    <topicmeta>\n      <keywords>\n        <keyword>3.4</keyword>\n      </keywords>\n    </topicmeta>\n  </keydef>\n  <keydef keys=\"download-url\" href=\"https://www.audacityteam.org/download/\" scope=\"external\" format=\"html\">\n    <topicmeta>\n      <linktext>the Audacity download page</linktext>\n    </topicmeta>\n  </keydef>\n  <keydef keys=\"project-extension\">\n    <topicmeta>\n      <keywords>\n        <keyword>.aup3</keyword>\n      </keywords>\n    </topicmeta>\n  </keydef>\n</map>"} lang="xml" title="keydefs-product.ditamap" />
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Read the elements</strong>
      <ul>
      <li><code>&lt;keydef&gt;</code> is a <code>&lt;topicref&gt;</code> specialized for defining keys. <code>@keys</code> is the key name. A <code>&lt;keydef&gt;</code> with no <code>@href</code> defines text only.</li>
      <li>The text comes from <code>&lt;keywords&gt;</code> inside <code>&lt;topicmeta&gt;</code>: the <code>&lt;keyword&gt;</code> is what a <code>&lt;keyword keyref&gt;</code> in a topic renders as.</li>
      <li><code>download-url</code> has an <code>@href</code> with <code>@scope="external"</code> and <code>@format="html"</code>, exactly as an external <code>&lt;xref&gt;</code> in stage 07. Its <code>&lt;linktext&gt;</code> is the text an empty <code>&lt;xref keyref="download-url"/&gt;</code> shows, because a web page has no DITA title to borrow.</li>
      <li>A keydef map is an ordinary map with a <code>&lt;title&gt;</code>. It is never published on its own; the guide references it.</li>
      </ul>
      </Step>
      </Steps>
      <h2 id="step-2-bring-the-keys-into-the-guide-and-key-the-topics" class="group scroll-mt-20">Step 2: Bring the keys into the guide and key the topics<a href="#step-2-bring-the-keys-into-the-guide-and-key-the-topics" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h2>
      <Steps>
      <Step>
      <strong>Edit </strong><code>audacity-guide.ditamap</code> After the <code>&lt;topicmeta&gt;</code>, add a <code>&lt;mapref&gt;</code> and one more <code>&lt;keydef&gt;</code>. Then add <code>@keys</code> to three <code>&lt;topicref&gt;</code> elements that are already in the guide. Save the map.
      <CodeBlock code={"--- a/audacity-guide.ditamap\n+++ b/audacity-guide.ditamap\n@@ -7,11 +7,14 @@\n     <shortdesc>Record, edit and export audio with Audacity, from your first track to a finished file.</shortdesc>\n   </topicmeta>\n \n+  <mapref href=\"keydefs-product.ditamap\"/>\n+  <keydef keys=\"start-here\" href=\"topics/what-is-audacity.dita\"/>\n+\n   <topichead navtitle=\"Getting started\">\n     <topicref href=\"topics/what-is-audacity.dita\" collection-type=\"family\">\n-      <topicref href=\"topics/what-is-digital-audio.dita\"/>\n+      <topicref href=\"topics/what-is-digital-audio.dita\" keys=\"digital-audio\"/>\n     </topicref>\n-    <topicref href=\"topics/installing-audacity.dita\"/>\n+    <topicref href=\"topics/installing-audacity.dita\" keys=\"install\"/>\n   </topichead>\n \n   <topichead navtitle=\"Recording and editing\">\n@@ -25,7 +28,7 @@\n   </topichead>\n \n   <topichead navtitle=\"Reference\" id=\"reference\">\n-    <topicref href=\"topics/supported-audio-formats.dita\" locktitle=\"yes\">\n+    <topicref href=\"topics/supported-audio-formats.dita\" keys=\"formats\" locktitle=\"yes\">\n       <topicmeta>\n         <navtitle>Audio formats</navtitle>\n       </topicmeta>"} lang="diff" title="audacity-guide.ditamap" />
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Read the map</strong>
      <ul>
      <li><code>&lt;mapref href="keydefs-product.ditamap"/&gt;</code> includes another map. Its key definitions join the guide's key space; a <code>&lt;keydef&gt;</code> that is not reachable from the root map defines nothing.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;keydef keys="start-here" href="topics/what-is-audacity.dita"/&gt;</code> gives a topic a key without adding it to the table of contents: a keydef is <code>@processing-role="resource-only"</code> by default.</li>
      <li><code>@keys</code> on a <code>&lt;topicref&gt;</code> that is already in the guide, such as <code>keys="install"</code>, keys the topic in place. The key space is <code>start-here</code>, <code>digital-audio</code>, <code>install</code>, <code>formats</code> and the four product keys.</li>
      </ul>
      </Step>
      </Steps>
      <h2 id="step-3-use-the-keys-in-the-topics" class="group scroll-mt-20">Step 3: Use the keys in the topics<a href="#step-3-use-the-keys-in-the-topics" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h2>
      <Steps>
      <Step>
      <strong>Key one topic first</strong> Open <code>topics/installing-audacity.dita</code>. In the download step, replace <code>Audacity</code> and the address after it with <code>&lt;keyword keyref="product-name"/&gt;</code>, then add <code>&lt;keyword keyref="product-version"/&gt;</code> and the link <code>&lt;xref keyref="download-url"/&gt;</code>. Do the same in the result, with the name and the version, and in the title. The listing in item 3 shows the finished lines. Leave the rest of the topic for item 3, and save the file.
      <p>The title and the download step now hold empty elements. Predict what the built page shows there. Then choose <strong>Project</strong> &gt; <strong>Check Project</strong>. The check covers the keys too, and it rebuilds the guide in <code>out/full/</code>. Open <code>out/full/topics/installing-audacity.html</code> and choose <strong>View</strong> &gt; <strong>Preview in Tab</strong>. The title reads <em>Installing Audacity</em>, and the download step reads "Download Audacity 3.4 from the Audacity download page", with a link to the download address. The words come from the key definitions, not from the topic.</p>
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Change the definition once</strong> In <code>keydefs-product.ditamap</code>, change <code>Audacity</code> to <code>Audacity Pro</code> and save the file. You have not changed the topic. Predict what its title and download step say after the next build. Then choose <strong>Project</strong> &gt; <strong>Check Project</strong> again, and preview the built install page as before. Every reference changed, including the title, <em>Installing Audacity Pro</em>, and the download step, "Download Audacity Pro 3.4". The table of contents shows the new title too. The short description still says <em>Audacity</em>, because it is typed text, not a key.
      <p>Change the name back to <code>Audacity</code>, exactly, and save the file. The rest of the course uses it.</p>
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Key the rest of the topics</strong> Every other literal "Audacity" becomes <code>&lt;keyword keyref="product-name"/&gt;</code>, and three links go through keys. In each topic, use <strong>Edit</strong> &gt; <strong>Replace...</strong> to find <code>Audacity</code> and replace it with the keyword. Skip the places that must stay literal, such as the Windows install folder, which is a file path, the web addresses, and the title of the quoted manual in <em>What is digital audio?</em>, which is an attribute value.
      <CodeBlock code={"--- a/topics/installing-audacity.dita\n+++ b/topics/installing-audacity.dita\n@@ -2,19 +2,19 @@\n <!DOCTYPE task PUBLIC \"-//OASIS//DTD DITA Task//EN\" \"task.dtd\">\n \n <task id=\"installing-audacity\">\n-  <title>Installing Audacity</title>\n-  <shortdesc>Download Audacity from the official website, or install it with your package manager, and launch it once to finish setup.</shortdesc>\n+  <title>Installing <keyword keyref=\"product-name\"/></title>\n+  <shortdesc>Download <keyword keyref=\"product-name\"/> from the official website, or install it with your package manager, and launch it once to finish setup.</shortdesc>\n   <taskbody>\n     <prereq>\n       <p>You need about 250 MB of free disk space and an account that can install software.</p>\n     </prereq>\n     <context>\n-      <p>Audacity is available for Windows, macOS and Linux.\n+      <p><keyword keyref=\"product-name\"/> is available for Windows, macOS and Linux.\n       The steps differ by operating system only at the install step.</p>\n     </context>\n     <steps>\n       <step>\n-        <cmd>Download Audacity from <filepath>https://www.audacityteam.org/download/</filepath>.</cmd>\n+        <cmd>Download <keyword keyref=\"product-name\"/> <keyword keyref=\"product-version\"/> from <xref keyref=\"download-url\"/>.</cmd>\n         <info>\n           <p>Select the installer for your operating system.</p>\n         </info>\n@@ -33,7 +33,7 @@\n           </chrow>\n           <chrow>\n             <choption>macOS</choption>\n-            <chdesc>Open the downloaded <filepath>.dmg</filepath> file and drag Audacity to your <filepath>Applications</filepath> folder.</chdesc>\n+            <chdesc>Open the downloaded <filepath>.dmg</filepath> file and drag <keyword keyref=\"product-name\"/> to your <filepath>Applications</filepath> folder.</chdesc>\n           </chrow>\n           <chrow>\n             <choption>Linux</choption>\n@@ -42,10 +42,10 @@\n         </choicetable>\n       </step>\n       <step>\n-        <cmd>Launch Audacity.</cmd>\n+        <cmd>Launch <keyword keyref=\"product-name\"/>.</cmd>\n         <substeps>\n           <substep>\n-            <cmd>If Audacity asks to scan for audio plugins, click <uicontrol>OK</uicontrol>.</cmd>\n+            <cmd>If <keyword keyref=\"product-name\"/> asks to scan for audio plugins, click <uicontrol>OK</uicontrol>.</cmd>\n             <info>\n               <p>This happens once, on first launch.</p>\n             </info>\n@@ -60,7 +60,7 @@\n       </step>\n     </steps>\n     <result>\n-      <p>Audacity is installed and ready to use.</p>\n+      <p><keyword keyref=\"product-name\"/> <keyword keyref=\"product-version\"/> is installed and ready to use.</p>\n     </result>\n     <postreq>\n       <p>Connect a microphone, then continue with <xref href=\"recording-your-first-track.dita\"/>.</p>"} lang="diff" title="topics/installing-audacity.dita" />
